## 12. Change-control procedure

Work in small, auditable steps.

First, inspect the repository and prepare the audit plan. Then implement only safe changes to scripts, documentation, and analysis outputs that do not overwrite original evidence.

Preserve unrelated working-tree changes. Do not commit or push anything.

If a change would modify original raw measurements, operate hardware, affect deployed services, install dependencies, or irreversibly alter user data, stop and ask for authorization.

At the end, report what was actually done, what was not done, and what remains uncertain.

The final objective is a scientifically defensible, reproducible manuscript whose strength comes from evidence quality, transparent methods, and accurate claims—not from unsupported numerical results.
